A||||10||camon||It's back!||||8||
Z||000000||camon||10-25-2001||11:25 PM||||Nice while it lasted but my squeal is back!<P>I posted about a week ago that my 87 GT had a squeal coming from the engine compartment. This squeal does not sound like a belt squeal and is most noticable while car is stopped. I think it is squealing even while car is in motion but can't hear it over the exhaulst. The sound kinda sounds like a power steering squeal (only it is constent and does not speed up or get louder while throttle is applied....it stays the same). What could be making this noise????<P>When I first heard it about a week ago I sprayed WD-40 on the alt. shaft (I thought maybe coolant leaked down on the pulley's) and I didn't hear it again until today. Well I tried WD-40 again only this time it didn't work. My belts are good and tight (as far as I can tell) and i'm not having any charging problems.....matter of fact the car runs great...but this squeal is very annoying and  I would like to figure out what it is coming from before I find out the hard way and end up walking home some night!<P>Could worn belts do this...how but oil pump? The water pump I replaced about 3 weeks ago so I doubt that is the problem!<P>Any idea's?????<P>Thanks Guys!!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||8
Z||000001||California Kid||10-25-2001||11:56 PM||||Sounds like (no pun intended) the alternator bearing is fried, or you haven't got enough tension on the belt.  Are you sure you didn't get WD-40 on the belt and contaminate it???||||reg||1
Z||000002||camon||10-26-2001||12:15 AM||||Well i'm gonna replace the belts this weekend just incase. As far as wd-40 on the belt(s). It is a possibility I quess but the noise was there prior to me spraying the alt. shaft. The WD-40 did cure the problem for about a week and now out of the blue it has returned. So maybe the bearings are fried....looks like I may need to take the alternator off and check it as well (it looks like a hard job though). Can the bearing be checked with the alternator still on the car (like shaft and pully play)? Hate to go through the headache of removing the alternator replacing the bearings (or  the  entire alternator) only to hear it didn't fix the problem. Ohh I hate it.......I just can't pin point where the noise is coming (just sounds like the general area of the belts and pulleys).<P>Question....will the oil pump make a noise if going bad? But that don't explain why it was silent for this past week after using the WD-40!<P>Thanks for the suggestion!<P>------------------<BR>Keith<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 10-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||10-26-2001||12:46 AM||||From the way your describing it, the WD-40 may have "lubricated" the belt, thus it wouldn't make noise, but still slip until the WD-40 wore off, when it would start to scream again.  Note: the oil pump will not make noise.<P>Suggest you try this: First take the belt off, then start engine and check for noise, don't let it run very long (no water pump). Shut engine down, if there was no noise, then check the alternator pulley to see if there is fore/aft or side to side slop when you try to move it. This isn't a sure fire way to check it, but it doesn't cost anything. You may even try turning it to see how it feels, again no exact science here.<P>If you really hosed the area with WD-40, I would think the belt and pulleys are contaminated with it. Best way to approach it is to get a new belt, clean the pulley with alcohol before you install it. Make sure you tension the belt properly, too little, it will scream, too much and you'll eat the bearings in your alternator much quicker.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000004||SLYGUY||10-26-2001||03:38 AM||||On the way home from the dealership I bought my GT at a howl developed that sounds ominously like the one you are describing. <BR>The sound didn't have a lot to do with engine rpm either. Sounded like some kind of turbine or something. I wasn't paying close enough attention to the guages. I was more concerned about temperature and oil pressure.<BR>That night the car cranked very slow a couple of times and then died. The battery was dead.<BR>Turned out to be the alternator. When I pulled it out of the car to take it with me to NAPA to match it up I spun the pulley and the same sound came from it. I bought a remanufactured higher amp alternator for $109. Battery charges well, engine cranks great and the sound disappeared along with that old alternator.<BR>Pull yours out and check it the same way I did mine. Replace it and be sure to put a new belt on clean pulleys. <P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||skitime||10-26-2001||07:03 AM||||Camon, If you want bring the car to my place and I can take a look.  I would like to see the car anyway.   <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||10-26-2001||07:40 AM||||You could always use the old long screw driver stethoscope trick to pin point the noise.||||reg||1
Z||000007||GodSend||10-26-2001||09:44 AM||||While changing out my thermostat last week I got a bunch of coolant to splash out of the thermostat housing.  Of course somewhere around my belts there is not a low grumble at all speeds (not a squeel at all).  I just replaced my belt about a month ago, and its nice and tight on there.<P>I think I may try to lube everything up down there.  Although I would not recommend wd40 as it will attract dust and just make it worse.  <P>Where abouts on the ALT did you spray that wd40?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||camon||10-26-2001||11:54 AM||||The WD-40 was sprayed on the little shaft behind the pully...I was trying to pay special attention not to get any on the belts (this doesn't mean none got on them). The main reason I did the WD-40 thing to begin with was to see if I could narrow down where the sound was coming from...or rule out where it wasn't coming from (seemed that after I originally sprayed it on the alt. shaft sound went away so I thought that was the problem...now i'm not sure.<P>Anyway alot of good advice here and I will need to do a belt change because they may be contaminated...and even if they ain't it couldn't hurt to put new ones one!<P>skitime: Yeah I could bring it up around your place sometime this weekend. Saturday or Sunday (which ever is best for you). I work Second shift during the week so it will need to be over the weekend!<P>Thanks again guys! <P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000009||grinthock||10-26-2001||12:02 PM||||BanditBalz has that problem on his 86GT and I have it on my 85GT, lasts about 30-60 seconds on startup then goes away COMPLETLY, Bandit has his alt ALL THE WAY back on his bracket, and it still does it.<P>Mine is almost all the way back, still does it, when I tried to make it tighter to get rid of the squeel, I bruned a belt, and then killed an alt, so the whine i just put up with||||reg||1
Z||000010||California Kid||10-26-2001||12:25 PM||||The alternator on the Fiero is very sensitive to belt tension, the rear bearing will fail if tension is set too tight. It will still operate, but make noise until failure is so bad it screams to be replaced. Had a failure on mine about 4 years back, the rear bearing (worn out) got so hot that it melted the aluminum housing pushing it off center, trying to relieve forces on the pulley (still was running though). <P>Summit racing does offer an alternator rebuild kit called the "iceberg" I believe. The kit contains a new rear alternator housing with a larger rear bearing, also new electrics with cooling fins. Claims are that it's allot stronger than GM setup and runs cooler.  Just thought I'd let you know about it, haven't done it myself yet, but plan to next time it goes out.||||reg||1
